# The encoder's PPM entry point: the cjpeg equivalent the byte-exact tests
# drive (ARCHITECTURE.md, "The two programs").
#
# Protocol: stdin gives the input path and the output path (natively
# `/file/<absolute path>`), then option lines until the end of input:
# `quality=Q` (default 85), `sample=S` (420, 422 or 444, default 420;
# ignored for greyscale) and `icc=<path>` (a file whose bytes are embedded
# as APP2 ICC_PROFILE chunks exactly as cjpeg -icc writes them, unchecked:
# it is the harness's input). stdout gets `ENCODED width height ncomp`, with
# the JPEG written to the output path; the exit status is 0. A refusal is
# a trap `REFUSED CODE: message` on stderr with exit status 1; any other
# trap is an encoder bug.
#
# P5 (greyscale) and P6 (RGB) inputs are both encoded, with maxval 255 or
# 4095: 12-bit samples are scaled to 8 bits by ppmread, so the encoder sees
# 8-bit rows either way. Rows stream from the loaded image into the row
# sink encoder.wack, which codes one iMCU row at a time.

: main ( -- )
  read-line drop :> inp
  read-line drop :> outp
  85 :> quality!
  420 :> sample!
  "" :> icc!
  1 :> more!
  [ more ]
  [
    read-line more!
    :> line
    more
    [
      quality line options.quality quality!
      sample line options.sample sample!
      icc line options.icc icc!
    ]
    when
  ]
  while
  inp ppmread.load :> im
  im pimage.w :> w
  im pimage.h :> h
  outp w h im pimage.n quality sample icc encoder.open :> e
  w im pimage.n i32.mul :> rb
  h [ :> y  e  im pimage.data y rb i32.mul rb bytes.slice  encoder.row ] times
  e encoder.close
  "ENCODED " im pimage.w i32.to-str str.concat " " str.concat im pimage.h i32.to-str str.concat
  " " str.concat im pimage.n i32.to-str str.concat
  println ;
